@page "/kilder"
@using ModernHost.Components

<PageTitle>Modern host — kilder</PageTitle>

<div class="hostchrome">
    <p class="hostchrome-note">
        <code>KildeSearch</code>, the kilde half of the catalogue on its own.
        <code>VariableSearch</code> is on <a href="/">the front page</a> — the two are separate
        entry points, not two tabs of one thing, so each gets a page here.
    </p>
    <p class="hostchrome-note">
        <strong>This is the compose-your-own route, not the mount a host normally wants.</strong>
        <code>KildeExplorer</code> is the whole kildeutforsker — the same list with
        <code>?kilde=</code> in the address bar and the handover already wired — and
        <code>samples/LegacyHost</code> and <code>samples/HostileHost</code> mount that. This page
        keeps the bare component alive instead, because a host that owns its own address bar has to
        be able to mount it, and because the wrapper below is what the handover looks like when the
        host wires it by hand. The wrapper owns <code>?kilde=</code> and
        <code>?datasamling=</code> here too, which is the rest of that job: reading them at
        initialisation, rewriting them, and remounting on a navigation the router intercepted.
    </p>
    <p class="hostchrome-note">
        <strong>The component is behind a wrapper here, not mounted straight onto the tag</strong>,
        and that is forced rather than chosen. <code>KildeSearch</code> raises
        <code>ExploreVariablesRequested</code>, and an <code>EventCallback</code> does not survive
        being passed from a statically-rendered parent into an interactive island: it is
        serialised as <code>{"HasDelegate":true}</code> and read back as an empty delegate, so the
        handler never runs. <code>@@rendermode</code> on the component tag does not fix that — it
        makes the mount point interactive while the parent creating the callback stays static.
        The callback has to be created <em>inside</em> the interactive component, so the wrapper
        is the island. <a href="/">The front page</a> mounts straight onto the tag and is right
        to: it passes only data, and data crosses the boundary intact.
    </p>
    <p class="hostchrome-note">
        <strong><code>ShowAccessAndPrices</code> is on here and off in LegacyHost</strong>, which
        is the difference between a host of Munin's own and a host that has its own pages for
        the same thing. The two blocks it draws over an open kilde — Kriterier for tilgang til
        data and Priser — point at helsedata.no, so an embedding on helsedata.no would be
        publishing a second copy of pages that site already owns. It defaults to off for exactly
        that reason: a host has to ask.
    </p>
    <p class="hostchrome-note">
        <code>prerender: false</code> is separate, and not a preference either:
        <code>KildeSearch</code> fetches the whole kilde list when it initialises, and
        prerendering would run that call twice against the API on every page load.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="hostframe">
    @* render-mode is set HERE, by the host, on the wrapper that owns the handover — see the note
       above for why it cannot go on the KildeSearch tag inside it. The package's own components
       stay render-mode free either way, which is the rule that lets them mount in LegacyHost's
       MVC view too. *@
    <KildeSearchWithHandover Language="no"
                             ShowAccessAndPrices="true"
                             @rendermode="@(new InteractiveServerRenderMode(prerender: false))" />
</div>
